<section class="page-hero page-hero-simple storage-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">FROM HARVEST TO TABLE</span><h1>Keep the fruit.<br><em>Keep the flavour.</em></h1><p>Fruit quality changes after harvest. Gentle handling, clean preparation and suitable storage can help preserve texture and reduce waste.</p><a class="button-primary" href="/guide/fruit-storage-and-food-safety">Read the full handling guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="simple-hero-side"><span class="big-serif-mark">✦</span><span class="side-rule"></span><p>Ripen · cool · protect<br>Use crop-specific guidance</p></div></section>
<section class="section-block"><div class="section-heading-row"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE PRACTICAL BASICS</span><h2>Storage starts with the fruit</h2><p>These rules are general starting points. Exact temperatures and ripening behaviour differ by species.</p></div></div><div class="storage-step-grid"><article class="storage-step"><h3>Check before storing</h3><p>Separate bruised, punctured or mouldy produce from sound fruit. A damaged piece can deteriorate faster and affect nearby items.</p></article><article class="storage-step"><h3>Know how it ripens</h3><p>Some fruits soften after harvest; others need to reach maturity on the plant. A familiar colour is not always a reliable signal of ripeness.</p></article><article class="storage-step"><h3>Cool at the right time</h3><p>Refrigeration can slow deterioration for many ripe fruits, but cold-sensitive tropical produce may suffer chilling injury. Follow species-specific advice.</p></article><article class="storage-step"><h3>Handle cleanly</h3><p>Wash whole fruit under clean running water before cutting. Use clean knives and boards, refrigerate cut fruit promptly and avoid leaving it warm for long periods.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="storage-feature"><div><span class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">A LITTLE PLANNING HELPS</span><h2>Buy for the week,<br>not just the basket.</h2><p>Combine fruit that is ready to eat with firmer fruit that needs time. Freeze surplus ripe fruit for smoothies or cooking, or preserve it with a method suitable for the crop.</p></div><div class="storage-feature-note"><strong>Remember</strong><p>“Room temperature” and “refrigerate” are not universal instructions. Variety, maturity, room warmth and local food-safety guidance matter.</p><a class="light-link" href="/guide/how-to-judge-ripeness">Learn ripeness cues <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
<section class="section-block"><div class="section-heading-row"><div><span class="eyebrow">QUICK CHECKLIST</span><h2>Before you eat or serve fruit</h2></div></div><div class="checklist-panel"><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Confirm the fruit is correctly identified, especially if it is wild or unfamiliar.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Remove damaged pieces and check for mould or unusual deterioration.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Wash whole fruit with clean running water before slicing.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Use a clean board and knife, and refrigerate cut produce promptly.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Follow crop-specific advice for fruits that need special preparation.</span></label></div></section>
